Each episode in this series will explore one crucial topic and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:02,160
With me today, Eric Suffert, media strategist, quantitative marketer, author and investor.

10
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:06,960
Eric currently shares his musings in the Mobile Dev Memo podcast, newsletter and blog.

11
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:19,280
On the podcast, I talk with Eric about why app economy disruption won't happen as fast as everyone seems to think how AI is just as useful for defending against copycat as creating them.

12
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:25,040
And why the real barrier to app success is still distribution, not code.

13
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:27,760
Hey, Eric, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,960
David, good to see you.

15
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:37,920
Hardly a day has gone by in the past few months where I don't have someone asking me what's the future of the app economy.

16
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:47,760
You know, freaked out that AI is going to consume all apps, that you're only going to have chat GPT, that competition's crazy, that, you know, all manner of questions.

17
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:52,880
So I have, gosh, like, six or seven of the top questions I get get asked on a regular basis.

18
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,120
And I wanted to chat through these with you.

19
00:01:55,360 --> 00:02:02,000
I think of all the folks around the industry, you are kind of the foremost app economy expert.

20
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:06,800
And I know you're also super into AI these days and following the industry very closely.

21
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,520
So I thought it would be great to have you on to get your perspective on all this.

22
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,160
So let's just start with that first question.

23
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:16,000
Like, you know, it's so much easier to build an app these days.

24
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,480
People are vibe coding apps in a afternoon.

25
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:22,400
What does a competition angle mean for the app economy?

26
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,080
Well, first of all, thanks for having me.

27
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:25,840
I always appreciate the opportunity to chat with you.

28
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,240
Yeah, I mean, it's a good timing because, you know, we had.

29
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:09,840
years on Mobile Dev Memo, but I made this point, I don't know, 2023, 2022.

30
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:19,760
A lot of these generative tools are inflationary for the app economy and for advertising in general because, you know, as you just get more and more content being published, it becomes harder to stand out.

31
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,760
I mean, that's just like a pretty fundamental premise.

32
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:23,760
I mean, I don't think anyone would disagree with that, right?

33
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:34,960
And, you know, I wrote a post on LinkedIn about this some time back because, you know, I saw a bunch of people making predictions around the start of the new year about, like, mini apps and how these would take off, and it's so easy to start an app.

34
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:42,480
Why don't you just, you know, everyone will just create the apps they need and these mini apps will become the kind of dominant interaction paradigm and, you know, they might.

35
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,080
look, they might, but you still have to get people to discover your stuff.

36
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:52,160
And if there's a lot more stuff from which to stand out, you have to work harder to stand out.

37
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:55,680
And that means advertising, most likely.

38
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:08,320
And if you look back at the kind of history of these mechanisms that made publishing easier, right, and in the piece that I talk about, I talk about the Directipe photography that made it easier to kind of get images in front of people.

39
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:17,120
But like if you look at this kind of more recent history of just blogging, right, the app store itself, right, when has user acquisition become easier over time since 2008?

40
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:17,600
Has it ever?

41
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:19,600
There's just there's there's there's there's this long history.

42
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:23,120
of, well, if it gets easier to publish stuff, it gets harder to get people to discover it.

43
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:28,480
At a more fundamental level, does AI change our relationship to apps?

44
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:44,320
So will people, will someone just say, Hey, ChatGPT, I want to run a 5K, and ChatGPT is just going to coach them through running a 5K instead of downloading Runa or Strava or Couch the 5K or, you know, any of the hundreds of running apps out there?

45
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:46,080
That's a really interesting question.

46
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,480
I think it's certainly a very relevant question.

47
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:49,600
I think it's TBD.

48
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,080
But I think there's a bigger question there, which is like, are you saying, Hey, ChatGPT?

49
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,600
Who are you saying hey to, right?

50
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:01,040
Because that's not necessarily settled science that ChatGPT just wins the category, right?

51
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:05,280
And so the question is like, do you just sort of, is it Turtles all the way down and up, right?

52
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:07,200
Like, where do you go for that information?

53
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:12,480
Is it ChatGPT or does someone unseat ChatGPT as the go to verb for discovery, right?

54
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:23,200
But my sense is, look, I mean, you remember all of Apple's promises two DCs ago, where they rolled out Apple Intelligence, they talked about App Intense, and nothing of that came to pass, right?

55
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,400
They talked about this overhauled Siri, nothing of that came to pass.

56
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,880
Now, that's not because it's fundamentally impossible, that's just because Apple didn't do a good job on execution.

57
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,040
But like, I don't know if you remember with the App Intense stuff.

58
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:36,640
And they mapped out this whole series of interaction flows.

59
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:39,360
And the reason I remember this is I just remember writing about it.

60
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,600
So I just looked it up before we started talking because I wrote a whole piece about like, will this disrupt the app economy?

61
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:44,960
Because that's the same question people were asking.

62
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,000
If you have app intense, do I need to interact with the app at all?

63
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:53,760
Like, if I need to do something in that app, I'll just ask Siri, it'll go in, it's got you have the app intense, it'll hook into those and it'll do everything for me.

64
00:05:54,080 --> 00:06:08,640
And what I, you know, sort of hypothesized was like when this comes to fruition, it will at some point, it's like, yeah, maybe the category winners just run away with everything because I'm not going to say, hey Siri, open up one of my ride sharing apps and book me a ride to the movies.

65
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:11,760
I'm going to say, open up Uber because that's the dominant one, right?

66
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:15,520
And so that could become, you know, that could become the new dynamic that we play in.

67
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:21,600
But, but I don't think, I don't think we're going to end up is like some sort of agent that orchestrates everything for us.

68
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:26,800
And there's no UI in any app because it's all just APIs or just like MCP interfaces.

69
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:28,080
I don't think we're going to end up there.

70
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:36,160
And like your question about like with Strava, my sense is like the kind of chat bot interface that we see now is limited in a lot of like really fundamental ways.

71
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:40,720
And at some point, you know, people just prefer things that are more tactile and more interactive.

72
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:48,560
Now maybe ChatGPT could replicate that, but like that feels like a standalone interaction use case that you'd have to construct.

73
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:55,200
And I don't know that, you know, certainly not in the current interface, but I mean, this stuff changes all the time, but like, could they just have an interface for everything?

74
00:06:55,440 --> 00:07:01,120
And the running is, you know, learning how to run or whatever tracking you're running is one and cooking is another and, you know, whatever.

75
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:07,520
My sense is like that probably ends up not being super meaningful for them in the same way, like, why didn't Facebook do that?

76
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:12,320
Because ultimately, like, you're going to have your core business and that's going to dominate everything.

77
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:18,800
And so building out all these like use cases of specific like functionality are not going to be valuable enough for you to even do.

78
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:38,160
And I think people, you know, the the the the the the kind of AI takes over everything bulls are underestimating the productivization that takes place that all the people at Strava who have been building this product over the years and have that knowledge and have new features they're continuing to add on in a weekly, monthly cadence.

79
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:39,840
You just don't get that.

80
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:49,120
And so at some point, ChatGPT or some LLM is a better product manager than human product managers and it can just like build the perfect interface.

81
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:52,720
But I think we're a long way from that being true.

82
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:54,480
But then what about like vibe coding?

83
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:58,000
Are people just going to vibe code their own 5K app?

84
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:00,880
No, I mean, that's going to be like a hobbyist effort.

85
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:06,640
I mean, the thing is, you know, coding has never really been the core limitation of getting an app done, right?

86
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:09,200
It's, you know, and I mean, you'll see this.

87
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:16,400
I mean, like, yeah, you could probably vibe code like a recipe app or something that works just as well as anything you get off the internet, but even then probably not, right?

88
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:23,040
Because like, it's just specialization and it's managing the edge cases and it's having, you know, gotten user feedback.

89
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:26,080
I mean, if you're just vibe coding something from scratch, it's not going to be very good.

90
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:36,000
I mean, you're going in there with just your intuition versus like an app that's successful in the app store is actually operating on this long history of feedback, right?

91
00:08:36,160 --> 00:09:17,920
And that didn't really, I mean, none of those games were commercially successful.

92
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,800
And in, and like, here's a good point that I see made a lot, right?

93
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:21,520
It's not mine.

94
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,680
Name one successful company that where their product was built by Lovable.

95
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:26,640
Name one single one.

96
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:31,200
Now, maybe there's a bunch of people using their own recipes app that they built with Lovable.

97
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:32,400
Can you name one single company?

98
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:36,320
And Lovable apparently, according to them, has a lot of ARR.

99
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:40,960
I don't know what words they're substituting in for that acronym, but they say it's ARR.

100
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:46,080
But name me a company that is successful that is built on Lovable.

101
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:47,600
And I don't think I can name one single one.

102
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,640
We're so early, so it'll be interesting to see.

103
00:09:50,960 --> 00:10:00,240
Because I do think there is potential for someone with good intuition and a good idea to go into Lovable and build something that becomes a great app that a lot of people use.

104
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:03,360
So I don't think that's outside of the realm of possibility in the future.

105
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:07,120
But to your point, so far we haven't seen any real breakouts.

106
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:15,360
What we've seen is people who are already building apps and already had that product intuition and do have some amount of code experience.

107
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:18,160
They're the ones really actually succeeding.

108
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:22,720
And then to your earlier point, it's also the ones who figure out distribution.

109
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:24,400
It's not just about the code.

110
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:32,320
And so, yeah, you can pull something together in Lovable, but how are you going to actually get it in front of in front of people and that's still such a huge barrier to entry.

111
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:42,080
I do push back and like maybe someone would say like, okay, boomer to this, but I just push back fundamentally on the idea of vibe coding like an enterprise grade or a scalable product.

112
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:43,200
I just don't think that's possible.

113
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:45,120
And I don't think that's ever going to change actually.

114
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:50,320
I mean, you just get, you just encounter so many dependencies with writing code and it has to be done really thoughtfully.

115
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:51,120
I mean, there's a reason.

116
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:56,400
If you talk to like a senior software engineer, if like they publish two hundred lines of code in a day, that's like a big day, right?

117
00:10:56,720 --> 00:10:58,720
The biggest part of the job is not writing the code.

118
00:10:58,960 --> 00:11:03,680
It's actually thinking through the implications on the code base of what you're publishing.

119
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:04,480
Is it scalable?

120
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:07,040
Is it going to break something else?

121
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:09,200
Like, is this going to have to be rewritten in three years?

122
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:10,320
Is it extensible?

123
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:17,920
And, you know, I don't vibe code in the sense that I don't go into cursor and tell it to, you know, build me an app.

124
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:22,720
That wouldn't be a scalable thing that, you know, could build you like, and you hear this said a lot.

125
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:27,840
I mean, this is, again, this is not like some novel insight, but you can you can build an MVP really quickly with these tools.

126
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:33,120
To build something that is stable, that is scalable, that is extensible, that is really difficult.

127
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,440
And so I use, I use, now don't get me wrong.

128
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:42,160
I mean, I use AI tools all the time for coding, but I want to see and touch every single line of code that goes into a big code base.

129
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:43,920
And I maintain a couple.

130
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,680
And what I do is I'll have it write a function for me, right?

131
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,440
I'll have it review code, right?

132
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:53,760
But if you talk to anyone who actually writes software, ask them what the most fulfilling part of their job is.

133
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:54,800
It's deleting code.

134
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:56,240
It's getting rid of code.

135
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:11,760
It's getting rid of old junk and knowing that you can get rid of it without breaking something and that, like refactoring in that way, you know, what you see a lot of people doing with, like, vibe coding is they'll just start the whole code base over that clearly doesn't scale for a large enough, like, a large enough code base.

136
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:16,240
A, it's going to be more expensive than just having a software engineer working on it.

137
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,640
But, you know, you're just going to be burning tokens all the time.

138
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:26,560
But, B, then you're just just going to have to do that again and again and again, and you're not going to have the consistency of a code base because some things are going to change each time you do that.

139
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:34,720
If you're going to build a meaningful app, these tools as they exist today, and they will get better, but they're not going to support that kind of scalability.

140
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:36,560
They're not going to support that kind of uptime.

141
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:45,040
Yeah, and it just takes a lot more than is still assumed in the AI hype cycle as of today to actually build that kind of great stuff.

142
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:49,200
So, but, you know, things are moving fast, so we'll see how much better things get.

143
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:56,720
As things do move quicker and quicker, though, the one thing I have heard a lot of people asking about is copy cats, is that, you know.

144
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:00,560
that a skilled developer using AI can move faster.

145
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:12,080
And so ripping off an app, like the entire interaction paradigms, I mean, some are even just ripping off style and like assets from the apps and stuff like that.

146
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:22,480
How do you think about copycats moving forward, that if you have a success within a few days, there's going to be tens of competitors for any app that goes remotely virable?

147
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:23,520
A couple of things, right?

148
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:30,960
So one is that that's where true design intuition tends to save you and tends to differentiate you.

149
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:33,840
And, like you said, I mean, people are just copying these features.

150
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:34,560
or whatever.

151
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:41,040
And so, like, well, then people are just piggybacking on your true, you know, your sort of inherent design instinct or skill that you've built up over a long time.

152
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,480
It doesn't necessarily look at an instinct.

153
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:44,240
They can't build new features that way.

154
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:44,560
Right.

155
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:48,960
I think that ends up differentiating quality experiences from the mass.

156
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:49,440
Right.

157
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:54,160
If someone's just copying what you've done pixel by pixel, there's not much you can do to defend against that.

158
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:57,040
You see this a lot now with creatives, right?

159
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,120
I mean, this has been a problem for years, right?

160
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:01,600
Like, if you have a winning creative, it's copied the next day.

161
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:02,480
And so what do you do?

162
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:06,800
You've got to build a process to always have a winning creative that just shrinks your lead time.

163
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:08,480
And that's just a fact of nature.

164
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,800
And like that didn't stop user acquisition.

165
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:14,640
In fact, it just made it much more important to have a scalable process, right?

166
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:17,600
A actual repeatable process for producing winners.

167
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,840
And that, what that ended up doing was just accelerating like the velocity of stuff that you need.

168
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:25,680
But I think it also made it a lot more process driven, which is not possible to replicate.

169
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:30,240
Like if you're a solo dev, you can't really replicate a process because you don't see it.

170
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:35,360
You can replicate the ad, but you can't replicate the process, which means you'll always be eating the crumbs.

171
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:39,200
And that's where, again, you see the primacy of distribution, not production.

172
00:14:39,520 --> 00:14:43,440
I have been really encouraged to see Apple taking a hard stance on copycats.

173
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:47,760
They updated the rules of the App Store recently, you know, saying copycats will be banned.

174
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:54,960
I've heard from several developers who had blatant rip off apps where Apple very quickly took them down.

175
00:14:55,280 --> 00:15:03,200
So I do think that there will be some onus on the platforms and this is where sometimes the platforms can be helpful in being able to police some of the stuff.

176
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:08,160
I mean, it's not going to be perfect and, you know, folks don't always just completely rip you off.

177
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:10,400
They take, you know, aspects of the idea.

178
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:13,920
But yeah, it's nice to see the platforms getting involved in that way as well.

179
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:21,600
And that provides a little bit of defensibility, I think, in a healthy way, not preventing competition, but preventing just being ripped off.

180
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:30,160
There's another piece of this too, which is like the AI stuff., it's not one side, it's not just the copycat and the scammers and the fraudsters that benefit from AI.

181
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:33,920
I mean, there's the other side of that is using AI to defend yourself against that kind of stuff.

182
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:37,760
So you can use AI to scan the app store every day, look for copycat apps.

183
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:40,000
I mean, we've built stuff at Fabulous to do this.

184
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:45,520
We look at the app store every day, we're looking at stuff that's rising virally, we're scanning it, we're looking at the ads that they run.

185
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:47,520
And so we have defense mechanisms for this.

186
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:51,520
So it's not just one side that the nefarious use case of AI always wins.

187
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:56,400
The last thing I wanted to touch on was wallet share and willingness to pay.

188
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:01,600
As software becomes easier and easier to produce, and it does.

189
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:04,880
seem like it's going to just continue to accelerate from here.

190
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:09,440
Is this deflationary on the entire software ecosystem?

191
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:15,040
Apps are cheaper and cheaper to build, so developers can charge less and less for them.

192
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:23,360
Do app prices go down as AI apps like ChatGPT and Cloud and Gemini and others take wallet share?

